videos.script:
On a laptop, a window stays where you put it. On a phone there is one screen, and every app is a pile of screens on top of each other. {{stack}}

How that pile is arranged is navigation. A stack, where each screen sits on the one before it. Tabs, where each tab keeps a pile of its own. {{tabs}} A drawer that slides in from the side, and a sheet that rises over everything for a moment. {{shapes}}

Then comes the one real difference between the two platforms. Every Android phone has a system back, a button or a swipe from the edge, and your app has to answer it. {{back}} An iPhone has none. Going back there is a button the app draws. {{ios}}

Today you model the planner's two tabs in a small program. You feed it taps and back presses, and print every stack after each one. {{run}} Watch the last press, at the very bottom of the pile. On Android, it leaves the app.
